# Career transcript — speak, don't write

Record yourself talking through your career and paste the transcript
here. Rambling is fine and actually better: karani wants the detail a
resume compresses away, not more polished summary.

Delete this guidance once you have real content.

## What to cover, per role

- What the company actually did, and what state the system was in when
  you arrived. "The ingestion pipeline fell over every Monday" is worth
  more than "worked on data infrastructure".
- The problem you were hired to solve, and whether that turned out to
  be the real problem.
- Decisions you made and why — especially the ones you argued for
  against resistance, and the ones you got wrong.
- Numbers you remember: throughput, latency, cost, team size, how long
  it took. Approximate is fine; say so.
- What broke. Incidents, the 3am page, the migration that went
  sideways, what you changed afterwards.
- Who you worked with and how: who you convinced, who you mentored,
  what you owned end to end.

## Also worth saying

- The through-line: what kind of problem you keep coming back to.
- What you want next, and what you will not do again.
- Anything the resume understates because it was politically awkward
  or hard to phrase in a bullet.

## Why this matters

Everything here lands in the cached half of every prompt, so it costs
almost nothing per application and gives the drafter real material.
Cover letters stop sounding like the job description read back.
